WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.976 --> 00:00:06.670
JULIEN BLANCHEZ: Good afternoon.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.160
For those of you who were
not there this morning,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.076
my name is Julien Blanchez.

00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:13.450
I've been nine
years with Google,

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.560
worked the last four years
alongside the engineering,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.440
security, and compliance
teams in California.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.130
I'm just back in Brussels
now, located from Brussels.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:30.160
And from Brussels I support the
engagement towards regulators,

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:37.420
towards large prospects or
very sensitive prospects--

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.400
prospects handling
very sensitively data

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.280
even if they're not large, and
I facilitate the communication

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.400
between the European markets
and the teams abroad.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.070
What I want to do
in this session

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.070
is go deeper into
the summary message

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.920
that I tried to compile into
10 minutes this morning.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.000
So sorry for those of you who
were there early this morning.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.780
There will be some repeats, but
in each time I will go deeper.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.290
And my objective is to share
with you some of the talking

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.080
points, some of the
narratives that we

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.880
use when we talk
to these people,

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.260
the senior decision-makers
that we need to help shift

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.380
and nudge in the
direction of the cloud.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.850
So there will be some of that.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.580
I will be addressing also
some of the key messages that

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.460
need to be heard in the market.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.270
I know I have more content
than that is needed.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.480
I try to keep it to
35 minutes and keep

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.700
10 minutes for questions.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.560
So as I talk and
you have questions,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.200
feel free to note
them down, and I'll

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.610
be happy to take questions.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.844
Even though this is a
tricky exercise in public

00:01:45.844 --> 00:01:47.260
with a large crowd,
there's always

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.200
someone that has the trickier
question than the other,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.910
but I'm happy to go
in that direction.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.850
So I will do this in two steps.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:04.540
The first step will be for
me to go into the technology,

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.139
and then I will cover more
the compliance side of things

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:12.070
because it's a very important
topic here in Europe.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:16.870
Before I start, I'm going to ask
you to do a trot visualization

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.710
exercise.

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:22.030
If you are in meditation, you
know what visualization is.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.300
You close your eyes,
and for a few seconds

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.220
you just picture for yourself
that one person in your family,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:35.500
in your close group of friends
that is still today saving

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.190
all of his money, his lifelong
savings under a mattress

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.030
in his bedroom or her bedroom
and possibly a shotgun

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.027
in the living room.

00:02:44.027 --> 00:02:46.360
And you close your eyes and
you think about that person.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.910
We all have that person
in our families or group

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.260
of friends behaving that way.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:58.890
Or maybe we don't because
nobody does that anymore.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.300
And the reason I'm
using this little trick

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.630
is because I want to
introduce a fact now

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.332
that an increasing
number of people

00:03:06.332 --> 00:03:08.290
are making a parallel
between what is happening

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.980
with the cloud today with
what has been happening

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.900
with the bank world and
the banking revolution 100,

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.660
250 years ago.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:18.340
What the banks did--

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:21.130
managed to do over a
quite long period of time,

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.340
they managed to convince
every single one of us

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.770
to take the most valuable
belongings they had away

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:34.870
from the silos of sparings and
bring this into what was then

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.550
called capital.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:41.200
And what capital allowed to
do was absolutely unthinkable

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.780
before capital was created--

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.950
motorways, skyscrapers, research
program, quest to the moon.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:53.110
You name it, this was all made
possible because of capital.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.720
What is happening with data
today and with the cloud today

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.070
is very similar.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.680
Gradually we receive
the guarantees,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.480
we receive the commitments.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.160
We grow up with the idea that
it is thinkable to take away

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.350
the information that is very
secure in these small silos,

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.110
and we agreed to
bring it together

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.200
in these big amounts of
data hosted in the cloud.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.140
And this allows
for ideas to happen

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:25.000
that were completely unthinkable
even a few months ago.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.460
I remember presenting
on driverless cars

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.680
in Europe three years ago.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.150
You had people
laughing in the room.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.780
Nobody laughs about
driverless cars anymore.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.420
And these things are
coming, machine learning,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:38.170
virtual reality--

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.450
this is all-- these are
all new technologies,

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.230
new ideas that are powered
by the cloud, made possible

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:49.450
by the cloud.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:53.230
The banks brought something
else than capital.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.890
They brought
security, and today we

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.610
cloud providers stand
bolder, I would say,

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.050
than they would stand
a couple of years back,

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:07.600
saying we can play a
similar role to what banks

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.530
have played when it
comes to increase

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.590
the security of the data.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:13.480
And there are a couple
of technical reasons

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.940
for this that I touched
upon this morning

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:19.030
that I want to go a little
bit deeper on this afternoon.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.210
Then as was mentioned
this morning,

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.590
you will all be there thinking,
it's all fine and good,

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.500
I understand Google has the
resource and the capacities

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:36.260
to protect the data, but
I still don't trust you.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:37.780
And so this is
where I will go more

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.730
into details about what
are the tools, what

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:43.150
are the clauses, what are
the information that you need

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.380
to engage into these
discussions with yourself,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.350
with your stakeholders,
with your customers

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:53.050
and see how long it takes but
progress in that direction

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:57.490
to decide whether or not you
want to move to the cloud.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.060
If I manage time well,
I will have the time

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:06.100
to share with you more tools,
maybe go a little bit deeper

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.830
into the tools that I quickly
snapped this morning that

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.850
can help your organization
be more compliant

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.410
and get ready for the
GDPR among others.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:20.530
I also want to share a
few of the personal tools

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:24.130
that you can activate right
now by the end of the day

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.750
or certainly during the
weekend on your own account

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:31.840
to significantly increase
your security posture,

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.090
and these are tools that are
available in the consumer

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:38.230
world of Google but are easily
activated on the organization

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:43.270
side, and so your administrator
or yourself can activate

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.480
this for your organization.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:54.700
This process of assessing deeply
the technical capabilities

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.170
in terms of security and then
building the trust and this--

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.540
within the stakeholders to
decide to move to the cloud

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.620
is not the process that
you will pioneer into.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.880
I don't know if this is English,
but that you will pioneer.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:14.140
Today Google serves 6
million organizations.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:18.670
Three million of them are NGOs,
educational organizations.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.580
The three other million are
commercial organizations

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.710
paying their licenses.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:26.380
And you recognize on
these slides already

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.260
names of companies that
are handling extremely

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.940
sensitive information.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.550
The one that we engage a
lot with at the moment,

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.120
we do joint events and we
try to evangelize is PwC.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:42.850
PwC is rolling out
100,000 to 200,000 users

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:46.420
at the moment across the
world, and they're handling--

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.490
how sensitive--
how more sensitive

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:53.890
can you be than tax information
from your customers?

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:56.950
You see names like
Charles Schwab, Deloitte.

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.010
"The Economist" is an
interesting example.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:02.650
The source-- the life and
death of some of the sources

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.160
being handled there depends
on the security of the data

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.260
and how well it is managed.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.440
When we present in
the south of Europe,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.320
I like to bring up the
example of "LeMonde."

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.940
"LeMonde" is one
of the dailies that

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.660
has the most critical
editorial line on Google,

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.950
and yet they are using
G Suite today to work.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.460
Airbus is another good example.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.570
There are some names that
are not on this slide.

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:34.320
And there's one in particular
that is a very good example,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.820
and normally we
don't talk about them

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.130
because we don't have
the marketing rights,

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:41.360
but they blogged about
it a few months ago.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.630
So I just now refer to the
blog and I encourage you

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:49.600
to Google UK Cabinet Office.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:55.320
So the team of the prime
minister of the United Kingdom

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:59.260
is using G Suite for
a year and a half now,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:00.830
and they are very
happy about it.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:04.030
They were so happy
they blogged about it.

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:07.720
And this is to give you an
idea of the level of security

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.580
and the level of
privacy commitments.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.740
These platforms in general--

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:12.700
these are all Google--

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.680
these are all Google
customers, but this

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.890
is the level of security
that cloud providers

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:24.170
have been able to give
now to their customers.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:31.120
So going into security itself
in the first part of the talk

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:35.470
and trying to understand
what it is that makes

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.260
cloud platform in such a--

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.110
puts Cloud platform in
such a positive spot

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.240
when it comes to security.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:46.090
I talked about this
model this morning,

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.160
assess the capability
to anticipate,

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.190
assess the capability to defend,
and assess the capability

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:52.240
to react.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.300
As a model that is widely used
across the security industry

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.580
to assess the security
of any system,

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.310
would it be an IT system
or a non-IT system.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:03.700
And I mentioned the fact
that Google operates

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.400
these seven platforms with
more than a billion users

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.940
allowing us to test these
platforms very, very broadly.

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.280
If you think of what
the business model has

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.470
been for the last
18 years of Google,

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.900
it has been to go
around the web and make

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:20.770
a copy of the entire
web for some parts of it

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.170
close to real
time, and this copy

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:25.090
is being brought to
our data centers.

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:27.520
It is being indexed,
and the search results

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.020
are delivered in a
few milliseconds.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:32.110
So of course, the indexing
doesn't happen on the live web.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:35.650
It happens on the copy, and
we deliver the search results

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.710
on this copy.

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:41.800
This gives you the
idea of how much

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.960
we know about what is
fantastic about the web

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.470
and that we share
with you immediately

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.250
in terms of search results.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.260
Also gives you an
idea of all that we

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:52.830
know that is terrible
about the web

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:54.820
that we also share with you.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.570
We share this in
specific services,

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.880
the most famous is
called Safe Browsing.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.340
It's a package service
in which we encapsulate

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.300
this data, this information
about the different websites

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:09.520
and what they contain
that could be harmful.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.230
And this service is shared
with Mozilla and Safari,

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.990
and so most of the web
browsers use that information

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:18.550
to protect you.

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:20.890
When you are going to
enter a harmful website,

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:23.530
you will receive a
message that tells

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:26.350
you caution if you
proceed beyond,

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:30.340
this could be harmful
to your computer.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.830
Normally when there are no
spots, I scan the audience

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.290
and I try to spot the
people going yes, yes, yes

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.887
because then I know who
is going on these sites,

00:11:37.887 --> 00:11:39.970
but here I have this spot
so I can't see anything.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.970
I talked about safety
nets this morning.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.670
Out of the 2 billion
Android devices, 400 million

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.220
polled daily to gather
the health information

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.540
and paint that landscape--
the threat landscape.

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:58.900
You might know that Google
Play is a fully fledged payment

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:02.410
platform completely
PCI DSS compliant

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:05.650
that handles the payment of
more than a billion users.

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:09.580
And Gmail serves tens
of thousands of doctors

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:13.300
and medical practitioners
around the world today.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:14.950
So it has to be compliant.

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:19.030
It had to comply with
very stringent health care

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.120
regulation; namely, the HIPAA
regulation in the United States

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:23.680
that is very demanding.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:29.400
So this gives you an idea of how
this platform is being tested

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.824
and allows us to gather
so much information.

00:12:32.824 --> 00:12:34.240
Out of all that
information, there

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:36.520
is one clear direction
that is important to keep

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:38.680
in mind if you try
to figure out what

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:43.019
is the direction that the
Google security teams are--

00:12:43.019 --> 00:12:45.310
one of the important direction
that the Google security

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.890
teams are trying to follow.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.980
And it is summarized
by this slide.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:55.240
It's public information, but we
have similar data in our logs.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:57.730
Almost all successful
incidents make

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:02.650
use of a vulnerability that
is older than one year.

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:07.870
That means that the problem
lies with us users--

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.900
us intermediate
users, administrators

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:13.570
being overwhelmed with
the complexity of having

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:16.780
to constantly update patch,
keep everything updated

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:18.580
and under control.

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:20.710
And even if your teams
and your security teams

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.800
had an infinite budget and
access to great research

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.890
resources and hire the
best security researchers

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.540
in the country or
in the world, you

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.210
know you're fighting
an uphill battle

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:34.690
because 2/3 of the incidents
make use of login credential

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:42.940
that have been shared
by your users willingly.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:46.630
And the reason for this is
according to this public data,

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:48.790
it requires less
than eight emails

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:52.630
to your organization to acquire
the login information of one

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:53.960
of your employees.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.330
We ran a survey in 2014 with
the University of San Diego,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.250
and our results for
the last data was 43%.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:03.260
There's actually less than
three well-crafted emails

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:08.090
to gather the login credential
of one of your employees.

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:12.070
So what does that mean
for large cloud platforms

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:15.050
in general and the mission that
the Google security teams--

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:16.966
one of the directions
that Google security has

00:14:16.966 --> 00:14:21.400
set for itself is that we
must use our capabilities,

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:23.380
the technical
capabilities that we

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:28.390
have to offload part of that
complexity from the user.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:31.780
We must resolve that
complexity problem

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:37.030
without imposing any
tradeoff on the convenience

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:40.360
that you have been used
to have on your devices

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:43.240
or on your systems
because we all

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.300
know that the least
tradeoff on convenience

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:50.500
that is being asked, you just
try to go around the security.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:53.410
I notice it with myself, I
notice that everybody-- it just

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:55.810
requires-- it's just
a couple of seconds

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.960
more that you need
each time you log in,

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:01.420
and then you will try to
avoid it at that threshold.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:04.720
So how can large cloud
platforms develop

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.150
systems that solve
this complexity

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:15.070
and avoid the tradeoff--
forcing for a tradeoff?

00:15:15.070 --> 00:15:17.869
So I touched upon
the basics here.

00:15:17.869 --> 00:15:19.660
I want to go a little
bit more into detail.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:21.201
I touched on the
basics this morning.

00:15:21.201 --> 00:15:23.030
I want to go a little
bit more detail.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:26.290
So these infrastructures
were built

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:31.180
for a blank slate, not having
to cope with the complexity,

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:34.510
but of course, if they grew up
being very complex systems--

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:36.280
I mentioned these
2 billion lines

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.560
of code and 50,000 engineer--

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.850
30,000 engineers creating
50,000 change requests per day.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:46.540
And I mentioned intelligent
automation, handling all this

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.490
and being able to
manage all this.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:51.070
If you want to
picture for yourself

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.170
a security problem
that has been solved

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:58.570
by that kind of
automation, you think

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:02.320
about how you were using
emails at the end of the '90s,

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.880
and you remember how
email was going to die,

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:07.450
email was unusable
because of spam.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:09.940
There was so much spam,
email was a dead tool.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:13.690
If you're using Gmail today,
you have less than 0.01%

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:16.270
of spam in your inbox,
and the reason for this

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:20.050
is that these systems
are reading your emails

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:23.727
and have become very, very good
at predicting what is relevant,

00:16:23.727 --> 00:16:25.810
what is not relevant for
you, what is threatening,

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.030
what is not threatening to you.

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.310
And when you know that
spam is one of the biggest

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:32.390
vectors for
distribution of malware,

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:34.360
this is a significant win.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:36.280
Actually one of the
wins I don't really

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.100
understand why we
don't talk more about.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.380
If you want to project
that into the future

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:44.560
and you want to think how
can this technology help me

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:48.050
in the future addressing some
of the big security issues,

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:51.820
think about authentication,
this very bad password hygiene

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:53.830
that we all have.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:57.340
Are we using the same password
across different websites?

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.410
I don't want to--

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:00.310
who does that?

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:01.150
Who doesn't do that?

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.474
We know that's a way
to solve this and one

00:17:06.474 --> 00:17:08.140
of the important ways
to solve this will

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:12.130
be behavioral
authentication, making sense

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.160
of the different signals that
we share with our devices,

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:17.619
with our watches, how
we look, how we sound,

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:20.064
how we handle your device.

00:17:20.064 --> 00:17:23.560
If we combine all these signals
together using these systems,

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:27.550
we're able to identify you by
a factor 10,000 more accurate

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:29.530
than a normal digit password.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:32.320
When we're able to
identify you so precisely,

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:35.050
we're then able to authenticate
you sometimes hopefully

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:39.130
in real time so that when I'm
using my mobile phone and I

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:44.770
access to call Google services,
my phone authenticates me

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:45.820
to these systems.

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:49.360
When I pass my phone to my son,
automatically the access rights

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:52.600
drop down to talk and go.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.340
This is the kind of technology
that is not future music.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:56.200
This is the kind--

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.387
proof of concepts are there,
SDK is there, and the trusted

00:17:59.387 --> 00:18:01.470
testers, and we are working
with partners of that.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:03.980
Of course, the road is long.

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.430
There's a lot of privacy
concerns, a lot of discussions

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:09.129
to have with the community
and with society.

00:18:09.129 --> 00:18:11.170
But these are the kind of
things that large cloud

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:13.840
providers and
Google specifically

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:17.800
will be embedding in their
technology as we grow, right,

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:22.000
and you will be benefiting
directly from this as we grow.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:27.490
I'm linking this slide with the
concept of security by design.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.880
It's fantastic that the
regulator imposes now security

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:31.420
by design.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:33.640
And when you will be
reviewing the GDPR,

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:36.370
you will see this term
"security by design."

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:39.450
Think already about
evolving this design,

00:18:39.450 --> 00:18:43.780
how can this design adapt
and evolve to the landscape.

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.590
This is what these
technologies will allow.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:49.050
The second concept that
I brought up this morning

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:53.280
was the control over
the entire stack.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:55.620
The biggest concern of--

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.540
if you ask a chief
security officer today--

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.190
and remember asking the question
to the chief security officer

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:00.690
at Google--

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.870
what is keeping him-- what is
keeping him awake at night,

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:09.240
one of the things that
he will bring up--

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.744
he or she will bring up is the
supply chain of the components,

00:19:14.744 --> 00:19:16.410
how are you sure that
the components you

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:19.590
bring into your systems in
the organization are secure.

00:19:19.590 --> 00:19:22.920
Because of the size of
that large cloud platform

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:27.690
and Google in particular, we've
been adamant on this topic--

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.640
because of the
size we operate at,

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.940
we have the luxury of
designing our own hardware,

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:37.890
our own chips, controlling
and designing our own custom

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:39.372
operating systems.

00:19:39.372 --> 00:19:40.830
And throughout the
different layers

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:43.020
of the stack all the way
to the mobile interface,

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.910
we have kept a very
high level of control.

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.490
I mentioned this
morning how this

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.970
allows for faster
or relatively faster

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:56.610
incident detection and relative
easier incident remediation.

00:19:56.610 --> 00:20:00.960
Again when you think of
the words of the regulator

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:05.280
today, breach notification,
how fast can you react,

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:09.090
having that kind of transparency
and homogeneity in the system

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:12.180
is something that will
play in your favor.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.140
Actually I want to talk
about this slide before.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:19.440
I will get to the
other one after.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:28.190
One of the important layers
here that is most illustrative

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:30.150
is the storage layer.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:35.090
How do we store the data, how
do we protect the data when

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:36.800
we store it?

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:39.140
Data at Google is--

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.420
when you enter
information in a Gmail

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:46.940
or in Google Docs, before
you even send the email

00:20:46.940 --> 00:20:51.110
or share the document, the
data that you have typed in

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:55.250
is instantly chunked into
pieces, several kilobytes

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:57.920
to a couple of megabytes
in size maximum.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:02.180
Each of these pieces is
replicated at least three times

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:04.970
on the same server
and at least--

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:07.550
or sorry, three times
in the same data center

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:11.450
and at least across two data
centers across the world.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.490
This is how it goes
all the time when

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:15.890
you use Gmail or Google Docs.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.350
Each of these chunks
of data is then

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.824
encrypted with a
data encryption key.

00:21:21.824 --> 00:21:23.240
The data encryption
key is located

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:27.114
very close to the chunks so
that it can decrypt very fast.

00:21:27.114 --> 00:21:29.780
But of course, there are so many
millions-- hundreds of millions

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:31.760
of data encryption keys.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.380
Each of these data
encryption keys

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:36.380
is encrypted with a
key encryption key.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:40.550
And the key encryption keys
are stored in the keystore,

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:42.580
and there are replicates
of the keystore

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:45.650
in approximately 10,000
locations around the world.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:52.100
All access to the keystore is
controlled by access control

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:54.760
list and logged.

00:21:54.760 --> 00:22:00.430
So it is-- we used to never
communicate this before.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:02.320
This is how we're
able to explain

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:07.660
to financial regulators and
other regulators of the world

00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:11.590
how we were sure that we're
in control of who is accessing

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:14.560
your data because it is
virtually impossible to go

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:17.230
through these two layers
of encryption without--

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:20.110
it is impossible to go through
these two layers of encryption

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:29.090
without accessing the keystore
and leaving a trace in the log,

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:32.810
Then I spoke about
the infrastructure

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:36.380
and how having your
data replicated

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:39.050
across a global
infrastructure, 15 data

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:41.870
centers across the
world, four in Europe--

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:44.840
I mentioned them this morning,
Finland, Ireland, Holland,

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:46.797
and Belgium.

00:22:46.797 --> 00:22:49.130
There's a slight competition
between Holland and Belgium

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:52.000
at the moment for who has
the biggest data center.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:54.500
So even at Google--

00:22:59.020 --> 00:23:02.450
and two more coming actually
very soon in Europe.

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.010
So an expansion of this network.

00:23:04.010 --> 00:23:05.750
But the size of
these data centers

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:08.600
is not the most important
piece of information.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:11.030
I find very relevant
information is

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:13.700
the connection of these dark
fibers that connect the data

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.530
centers, propriety fibers.

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:18.320
We lay out our own
cables through the ocean,

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:20.940
take part in the
consortium where we have

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:24.050
direct control on the cables.

00:23:24.050 --> 00:23:26.030
And that makes us--

00:23:26.030 --> 00:23:28.520
puts us in a very good
position to address latency--

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.290
I mentioned latency
is very important when

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:32.180
you offer a
Collaboration Solution,

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.580
but also in terms of
security we are very

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:36.530
much in control of our destiny.

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:42.080
For many of our customers, this
is a critical decision point.

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:45.290
We talk publicly about--

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:47.101
the Hollywood
industry is a big fan

00:23:47.101 --> 00:23:48.350
of Google Cloud at the moment.

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:51.170
They are using a lot of our
computing powers and storage

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:52.100
powers.

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:56.570
You can understand how sensitive
any movie is and they do not

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.670
want this to leak and
how aggressively they

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:00.860
can be attacked.

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:06.130
And having the benefits
of these encryption--

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:11.870
these propriety pipes through
the ocean self-encrypted,

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:16.260
encrypted under our own control
is very interesting to them.

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:19.050
So this is for the connection
between the data centers.

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:22.771
The yellow points that you
see there represent our Google

00:24:22.771 --> 00:24:23.270
front ends.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:26.510
So the connection points between
the Google infrastructure

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:28.320
and the public internet.

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:30.050
And this gives you
an idea of the amount

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:36.380
of options we can play with
when you come under DDoS attack,

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:37.100
for example.

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:41.360
It is then very easy and it
happens actually automatically

00:24:41.360 --> 00:24:46.250
to load balance the traffic
from any of those Google front

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:48.650
ends to make sure
that you remain online

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:54.540
even though one-half of the
world is being under attack.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:56.220
This is the kind
of infrastructure

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:59.220
that allows a large public
cloud provider like Google

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:04.150
to aim for what we called
zero recovery point objective,

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:05.820
so recovery point
objective of zero,

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:10.110
which means in case of major
catastrophe, zero data loss.

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:12.940
And zero recovery
time objective,

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.260
which means in case of major
catastrophe, instant failover.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.210
You do not see anything.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:21.210
Every year Google-- at
least every year Google

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.080
simulates a major catastrophe.

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:27.040
So we shut down
portions of the world.

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:30.205
You have never noticed
any of these drills.

00:25:30.205 --> 00:25:31.830
In the nine years
I've been at Google--

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:35.550
and I am 12 hours today
in front of these tools.

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:39.390
Once I was thinking wow,
there is something going on,

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.910
and indeed I never knew
exactly what it was,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:43.830
but there was something,
there was something.

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:48.180
So this infrastructure
are highly redundant

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:52.420
and are used to offer that.

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:55.380
Lastly and very important when
we switch to the compliance

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:58.080
discussion, this is
the infrastructure

00:25:58.080 --> 00:26:02.310
that allows us to aim to deliver
the same level of security

00:26:02.310 --> 00:26:04.740
for everybody in the world.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:07.230
You might be tired sometimes
of the idealistic stance

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:12.454
of Google, but this is very
important to the teams.

00:26:12.454 --> 00:26:14.370
There is no way that
we'll offer more security

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.100
to a German than to a
Pakistani or to a US citizen.

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:24.190
We want to offer the same level
of security for everybody.

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:27.120
And so whenever we hear you want
to have data here and there,

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:27.960
we like--

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.030
first of all, you might
not know what you wish.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:32.310
Locating data might not
increase your security.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:35.250
Secondly, this is
really not what we are

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:38.510
aiming to deliver to the world.

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:42.660
Quickly, another thing that
Google has been allowed to--

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:45.300
has been able to solve
is the biggest problem

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:47.670
of the chief security
officers today is

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.560
the access to human resources.

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.840
How do you convince the
right brains to come and work

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:54.450
on these tough problems
in your organizations?

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:57.680
Even if you're a large bank
in your country, of course,

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.570
there are several that
you will, but we have

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:02.280
a tremendous advantage there.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:05.910
And even though we have many
of these researchers in-house

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:08.430
working on these tough
problems, we actually

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:11.460
know that it is
not 700 people that

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:13.180
will solve security on the web.

00:27:13.180 --> 00:27:16.020
And so these guys are constantly
collaborating with the research

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.120
community and actually
for having worked

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.400
alongside these guys,
I realized they do not

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.680
come-- they did not
come to work for Google.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:26.280
They come to work for Dick,
John, who was their guru when

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:27.900
they were doing
their PhD, and they

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:29.870
want to keep on
solving that problem.

00:27:29.870 --> 00:27:32.700
If Dick or John goes
to another company,

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:33.990
probably they will follow.

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:37.780
The importance of this community
lives as a separate life,

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:41.087
and that's an understatement.

00:27:41.087 --> 00:27:43.170
So one of the things that
they are really proud of

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.270
is the vulnerability
reward program

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:49.140
where we actively encourage
anybody around the world

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:50.610
to attack our systems.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:53.130
Many of the questions we
receive from our institutional

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:57.060
customers is under what
conditions can we test you?

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.320
Any condition.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:00.640
It's all fair game.

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:02.170
Give it your best shot.

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:05.230
The email that we sent to
people that ask us this question

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.360
is literally three lines.

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:08.560
Give it your best shot.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.280
We don't want to know anything.

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:12.790
The only thing is if you find
a vulnerability, tell us.

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:15.080
Otherwise we can't pay you.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:18.536
We've paid already more
than $4 million in rewards.

00:28:18.536 --> 00:28:22.360
The researcher that received the
most money received $200,000.

00:28:22.360 --> 00:28:25.120
If they decide to
give this to charity,

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:28.030
we basically double the amount.

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:31.150
And believe it or
not, many of them

00:28:31.150 --> 00:28:33.807
give these things to charity.

00:28:33.807 --> 00:28:35.890
So this open collaboration
model is the last thing

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:38.080
I want to say because
in the world of security

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:40.310
it's a very important point.

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.880
This is so-- at
this point, I hope

00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:46.180
we have not exaggerated
on the technology aspect,

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:49.990
and we are not reaching
at the ad nauseum point.

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.110
I want to spin, to pivot--

00:28:53.110 --> 00:28:57.550
I want to pivot to
compliance and tell you

00:28:57.550 --> 00:28:59.950
what are the tools that
we give you to increase

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:01.970
that confidence in the system.

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:03.880
This is probably the
most important slide

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:06.946
of the entire presentation.

00:29:06.946 --> 00:29:09.070
The only thing you really,
really need to remember,

00:29:09.070 --> 00:29:13.570
if anything, is when you
sign a contract with a cloud

00:29:13.570 --> 00:29:15.650
provider-- whether it
be Google Cloud, Amazon,

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:16.960
Microsoft or any other--

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:21.070
you are being defined as a
data controller in control

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:23.020
of the data, and
the cloud provider

00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:25.360
is being defined as
a data processor,

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:28.990
and the data processor
may not use your data

00:29:28.990 --> 00:29:31.030
for any other purpose
than deliver you

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:33.190
the service that you paid for.

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.740
This is a cornerstone
of the GDPR, cornerstone

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:44.800
of all these contracts and
a very important notion that

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:48.970
also explains why there will
be no advertising by Google

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:53.140
or no usage by Google of the
data you put into our system

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:55.090
because it is
contractually written just

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:56.230
like in any other contract.

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:58.910
Before you write
into the contract,

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:01.750
you will have access
to all the information

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:04.120
that the regulators
now expect you to have

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:05.740
before you sign a contract.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:07.730
What is the exact
location of your data?

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:09.940
So you receive the
location of data centers.

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:12.130
We may not be able
to tell you in which

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:14.260
data center the
data is, but it is

00:30:14.260 --> 00:30:17.250
in some of these data centers.

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:22.450
The subprocessors-- who
are the subprocessors?

00:30:22.450 --> 00:30:24.670
The availability
of the systems is

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:26.190
available through
public dashboard,

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:27.898
so you can see at any
given point in time

00:30:27.898 --> 00:30:29.325
when these systems
are available,

00:30:29.325 --> 00:30:30.700
when they have
been down, and you

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:33.610
can make your own assessment
there, is it sufficient or not.

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:37.060
And importantly,
the certifications--

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:38.980
all large cloud
providers have undergone

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.930
now the major certifications.

00:30:40.930 --> 00:30:44.620
You will recognize, if you're
familiar to that world, 27001

00:30:44.620 --> 00:30:47.650
which certifies for
computing security,

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:52.870
2717 for cloud computing,
2718 for handling of BII.

00:30:52.870 --> 00:30:58.360
So these are the gold
standards on these topics.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:02.860
The second layer are the SOC
audit reports, very stringent,

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:04.360
very well recognized.

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:08.890
You can access-- we provide
you access to the SOC 2 report

00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:09.460
under NDA.

00:31:09.460 --> 00:31:11.320
So the SOC 3 is public.

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:12.070
You can obtain it.

00:31:12.070 --> 00:31:16.060
The SOC 2 is 210 pages
of granular controls.

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:18.490
It is also available for
our customers under NDA.

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:21.130
So if you really want to check
every single control, security

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:22.720
control, it is available.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:27.216
I just want to stop on to one
badge, which is the FedRAMP

00:31:27.216 --> 00:31:29.590
badge, because it's a clear
differentiator between Google

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:31.280
and the other cloud providers.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:33.580
All cloud providers,
major cloud providers

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:36.700
have achieved the certification
which allows cloud

00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:40.570
to deliver services to the
federal agencies of the United

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:44.050
States government,
including defense.

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:48.220
And so yes, we have many defense
customers using Google Cloud

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:51.490
and having the data
onto the infrastructure.

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:54.160
What is specific about
Google certification

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:56.800
is Google is the
only provider that

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.830
certified the entire
infrastructure as opposed

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.830
to our competitors who certified
subset of the infrastructure.

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:05.080
So they have built
specific farms

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:06.640
that have received the badges--

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:08.950
that badge, and these
farms of servers

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:12.160
that are serving
these federal agencies

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:14.440
and delivering that
kind of security.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:18.160
When you engage with Google, you
receive exactly the same level

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:21.130
of security that we guarantee
to Rockwell Collins,

00:32:21.130 --> 00:32:24.310
for example, a major defense
contractor of United States,

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:27.230
or [INAUDIBLE] for that matter.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:31.310
You have the information.

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.810
You get to the moment where
you signed the contract.

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:35.559
And there are a few
things that you really

00:32:35.559 --> 00:32:38.060
have to pay attention to.

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:41.390
The scope of processing
I mentioned absolutely

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:44.750
demanded by the regulators
today that the processor may not

00:32:44.750 --> 00:32:47.180
touch the data for any
other purpose, contractually

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:48.200
committed.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:51.950
Intellectual property, you
keep the intellectual property

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:54.589
of your data, seems
normal when you

00:32:54.589 --> 00:32:56.630
think about machine learning
and the intelligence

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:58.463
that you will develop
using machine learning

00:32:58.463 --> 00:33:01.490
tools on your big data sets,
very important that you also

00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:02.990
keep that knowledge
and that doesn't

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:05.030
come into anybody else's hands.

00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:08.780
Portability, when you have
developed 30 petabytes of data

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:11.870
in 10 years, how
do you walk out?

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:13.160
Are we going to help you?

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:15.050
How many trucks of
hard drives do we

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:17.744
have to bring to
the data centers?

00:33:17.744 --> 00:33:19.910
These are literal questions
that are being asked now

00:33:19.910 --> 00:33:22.220
with some prospects.

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:25.280
Very important is
the access to data

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:27.080
from third-party jurisdiction.

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:32.120
What happens if a foreign
judge asks for data

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:33.620
of one of your employees?

00:33:33.620 --> 00:33:37.580
You have to get back to
the concept of controller

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:39.200
and processor.

00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:42.470
In the world of the cloud
as opposed to the consumer

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:44.390
world of Google-- in
the world of a cloud,

00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:46.460
we are in a very
comfortable position.

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:49.020
When the judge comes
to us, we say, hey,

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:51.590
we are only processors.

00:33:51.590 --> 00:33:55.310
You should ask the controller,
and we redirect the request

00:33:55.310 --> 00:34:00.290
from the judge to you,
and you decide how you

00:34:00.290 --> 00:34:01.912
want to go with that request.

00:34:01.912 --> 00:34:04.370
Most of the time you will want
to collaborate with justice.

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:06.744
Sometimes you might not want
to collaborate with justice.

00:34:06.744 --> 00:34:08.090
It is really up to you.

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:11.060
The moment you feedback to
us a formal legal decision

00:34:11.060 --> 00:34:15.409
that you refuse, this is
where it stops for us.

00:34:15.409 --> 00:34:19.760
You keep on discussing with
the judge and with justice.

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:24.510
So, of course, there
are some French cases

00:34:24.510 --> 00:34:29.092
where we are not allowed to
tell your employee or you

00:34:29.092 --> 00:34:33.150
that we get requests
for the data.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:40.600
Pedophilia, organized crime,
drug smuggling, imminent crime

00:34:40.600 --> 00:34:42.580
into an investigation--

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:46.360
these are typical situations
where the lawmaker has provided

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:49.139
that there will be
no misstep to impede

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:51.159
into the execution of justice.

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:54.480
Generally the more regulated
the customer I engage with,

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:55.480
the more they know that.

00:34:55.480 --> 00:34:57.070
If I engage with
a bank and there

00:34:57.070 --> 00:34:59.050
are lawyers from the
bank in the room,

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:02.110
they just nod and
say yes, proceed.

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:04.810
We also have these-- probably
have these cases as often

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:12.790
as we do, and this is--
we are not above the law.

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:14.590
We do publish the
number of cases--

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:18.160
I need to hurry up.

00:35:18.160 --> 00:35:19.190
I'm out of time.

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:21.860
I hope I'm not too slow.

00:35:21.860 --> 00:35:24.220
We do publish all
the cases where

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:28.690
we get these requests in what we
call the transparency reports.

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:32.320
If you are interested in that,
Google transparency reports,

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:34.947
all large players have
done this by now also,

00:35:34.947 --> 00:35:37.030
and you can find transparency
reports from Twitter

00:35:37.030 --> 00:35:38.890
and other organizations.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:41.830
But you find by country,
by year how many requests

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:43.480
have been executed.

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:44.800
It's interesting when you--

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:49.000
I presented yesterday in Germany
and the Germans are very, very

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:52.210
adamant on how much America
can look into their systems,

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:54.920
and I pointed to
the report and said,

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.800
you should go and check
out because America

00:35:56.800 --> 00:36:00.430
made 13,000 requests
slides last year.

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:03.610
Germany made close to 10,000.

00:36:03.610 --> 00:36:08.680
And France, which is also very
reactive, made close to 9,000.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:10.660
So all of a sudden you
realize how important

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:16.060
it is to put your data
into an infrastructure that

00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:19.330
wants to defend everybody
the same way independently

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:24.910
of the local perception of who
wants to access what data when.

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:28.525
So security and our commitments
to keep the certifications

00:36:28.525 --> 00:36:31.150
that we talked about throughout
the duration of the agreement--

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:33.460
we don't just achieve a
certification, let you sign,

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:34.480
and then drop it.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:38.210
And of course, audit rights
is a very important topic,

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:40.720
depending on the sector
where you operate in

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:44.620
and your lawyers will be more
or less adamant on these close.

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:51.250
But in summary, these contracts
allow you to come and verify

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:53.350
that we do what we say we do.

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:56.200
Of course, we don't like to
have 6 million customers walking

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:58.186
across our data centers.

00:36:58.186 --> 00:36:59.560
So this is not
what is happening.

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:02.240
You will not have access
to our data centers.

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:06.610
But we have mechanisms
which allow you to check--

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:08.500
have access to the
detailed reports

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:10.132
and then propose
some new controls.

00:37:10.132 --> 00:37:11.590
If some controls
have not been done

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:13.420
and you're not satisfied
with the audit,

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:17.650
you can inject new controls that
will be included into the audit

00:37:17.650 --> 00:37:19.780
form for the next cycle.

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:23.740
At this point we have reached
the European discussion.

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:27.130
Will it even fit with
the European law?

00:37:27.130 --> 00:37:29.260
And here it's always
useful, I find,

00:37:29.260 --> 00:37:32.770
to summarize what the
European law actually says.

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:37.120
Still today the handling
of personal data in Europe

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:40.870
is regulated by a
directive of '95,

00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:44.380
1995 that very
specifically says it is not

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:49.570
allowed to export European
data unless adequate safety

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:54.990
measures are in place,
quoting the law, probably

00:37:54.990 --> 00:37:55.760
hopefully exactly.

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:02.350
Should have reviewed
the text before.

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:03.810
But these adequate
safety measures

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:06.780
are summarized in the
safe harbor, which

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:09.900
was invalidated last year,
replaced by Privacy Shield.

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:12.120
Privacy Shield is
heavily debated.

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.730
European activist
thinks it's not OK.

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:17.220
Trump Administration
thinks it's not OK.

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:19.290
At least these two
agree on this topic.

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:26.760
But it doesn't really matter
because all large cloud

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:29.880
providers, of course, rely
on Privacy Shield as one

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:31.950
of the mechanisms
that is fully--

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:35.220
gives the full title
to export data.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:39.630
But all cloud providers use
the model contract clauses,

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:42.780
and these are 12 clauses that
have been devised by the data

00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:46.110
privacy authorities of Europe
and they proposed model clauses

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:49.470
that all cloud providers
should add to their contract.

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:51.880
If these clauses are
added to their contract,

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:53.640
there is no more discussion.

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:57.840
You may export data, personal
data outside of Europe.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:06.720
So these model contract
clauses come in two shapes.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:11.850
I wanted to check what
slide I had behind,

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:14.670
and now you won't go back.

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:17.770
Either step model contract
clauses or model contract

00:39:17.770 --> 00:39:20.606
clauses that have been approved
by the common opinion process,

00:39:20.606 --> 00:39:22.980
and the common opinion process
is a process through which

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:26.870
the cloud providers proposes
these model contract

00:39:26.870 --> 00:39:30.540
clauses to the regulator,
and the regulator officially

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:33.660
approves these clauses
and they are binding.

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:36.624
And there is no way a data
privacy authority around Europe

00:39:36.624 --> 00:39:38.790
can then change his opinion
and say, oh, by the way,

00:39:38.790 --> 00:39:40.800
no, you can't use
Google, and you

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:44.160
have to keep data in my
country when you use Google.

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:48.690
This is a binding opinion,
including for Norway.

00:39:48.690 --> 00:39:51.960
Then comes the GDPR.

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:57.240
The GDPR will replace
the directive of 1995,

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:59.580
and it is very
important for all of you

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:02.190
to receive that commitment.

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:04.000
The deadline of
GDPR is coming fast.

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:10.070
By May 2018 this new
regulation will be enforced.

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:11.550
And what the GDPR does--

00:40:11.550 --> 00:40:16.950
it increases the power of
the data privacy authorities

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:20.220
to inspect what is being
done with the data, what you

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:23.190
do with your users'
data and your employees'

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:26.520
data and your customers' data,
what we do with your data,

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.580
and all of your
stakeholders' data.

00:40:29.580 --> 00:40:33.240
So the data privacy authorities
have gained a lot of power,

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:37.020
and they have also gained
very impressive tools

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:40.500
in terms of fines
that can go up to 4%

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:44.385
of the yearly global revenue
of your company per incident.

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:50.180
Some banks have many incidents.

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:53.700
Anything has many incidents I'm
not saying anything on banks

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:55.830
here.

00:40:55.830 --> 00:40:58.470
So a lot of emotions.

00:40:58.470 --> 00:41:01.650
In general on the positive
side, I personally

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:05.269
think that usually
Europe is always

00:41:05.269 --> 00:41:06.810
lagging behind the
United States when

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:09.420
it comes to digital revolution,
digital transformation.

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:12.370
For once that we do
something that really matters

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:15.390
and that really the rest
of the world is looking at,

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.880
we should be proud as
European of the GDPR,

00:41:17.880 --> 00:41:19.770
and we should
really embrace this

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:25.050
as an opportunity to raise the
trust of our entire society

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:27.840
in the digital world
and use this legislation

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:29.700
to accelerate adoption.

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:31.260
This is, by the way,
what we see now.

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:36.390
We see an acceleration of the
demand for cloud services.

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:39.540
Because of the increased
risk and some--

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:42.180
many stakeholders see
an opportunity, too.

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:46.100
Key for the GDPR is
that the GDPR does not

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:51.430
change the rules for
international data transfers.

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:54.360
So again, Google
Article 46 of the GDPR--

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.780
because this is very
emotional and people

00:41:57.780 --> 00:42:00.690
have completely different
opinions and ideas about it,

00:42:00.690 --> 00:42:02.160
the text is there.

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:05.580
Article 46 of the GDPR very
clearly states, a controller

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:07.920
or processor may
transfer personal data

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:10.149
to a third country or
international organization

00:42:10.149 --> 00:42:11.940
if the controller or
processor has provided

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:15.240
appropriate safeguards which
can take the shape of something

00:42:15.240 --> 00:42:17.280
like the Privacy
Shield, model contract

00:42:17.280 --> 00:42:21.620
clauses, or new mechanisms
like code of conduct

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:24.960
and certification mechanisms
that will come into place when

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:26.580
the GDPR is launched.

00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:30.060
And Google is actively engaged
into these other processes

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.880
to make sure that even if
Privacy Shield falls, even

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:34.750
if model contract
clauses would fall,

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:39.180
there would be other
mechanisms available.

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:43.080
I don't think I want to
repeat the advice I gave

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:46.800
this morning about
segmenting your decision

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:48.910
process into a technical
risk assessment,

00:42:48.910 --> 00:42:52.140
a legal risk assessment, a
political risk assessment

00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:54.540
is your country or any
of the countries you're

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:58.560
active in engaging
to data localization

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:01.040
legislation like
Russia, like China

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:04.950
and others in the Middle East.

00:43:04.950 --> 00:43:06.660
You need to have a
cloud provider that

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:09.510
can at the same time deliver
the service in these countries

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:12.810
and risk-- and protect your
information in these countries.

00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:16.890
And of course, the cultural
risk is the fourth one.

00:43:16.890 --> 00:43:20.400
You might be forced to
keep data in country

00:43:20.400 --> 00:43:21.690
because of cultural reasons.

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:26.040
This is perfectly
normal, but make sure

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.690
that you make a distinction
between these four layers

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:32.310
when you make this decision
because sometimes things

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:34.920
get confused, and
you might think you

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:37.830
might be taking the wrong
technical and legal decision

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:41.447
because of a cultural decision.

00:43:41.447 --> 00:43:43.530
I will go quickly over
these ones because I want--

00:43:43.530 --> 00:43:44.990
I touched upon
this this morning.

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:51.110
So I want to have more
time on the personal tools.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.850
Again, what I was trying
to convey this morning

00:43:53.850 --> 00:43:58.830
was in the areas
of responsibility

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:01.770
that you keep even in terms
of security and compliance,

00:44:01.770 --> 00:44:04.032
even as you move to the cloud--

00:44:04.032 --> 00:44:06.240
and you know, if you use
infrastructure as a service,

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:08.479
you still have a lot of
things that your customers

00:44:08.479 --> 00:44:10.020
and your regulators
will be expecting

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:11.490
you to do in terms of--

00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:14.070
you can benefit
from technologies.

00:44:14.070 --> 00:44:17.660
I spoke about authentication,
strong authentication.

00:44:17.660 --> 00:44:20.130
The security keys and
two-step authentication

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:26.040
are very important to easily
improve your security stance.

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:29.700
Encryption-- one of the
benefits of moving to the cloud

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:31.710
and moving to Google's
Cloud specifically,

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:34.500
you benefit from
encryption out of the box.

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:36.780
You can either trust
us to manage your keys.

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:39.600
If you don't, you can
manage your keys yourself.

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:42.630
And if you don't trust us to
even have access to the keys,

00:44:42.630 --> 00:44:46.410
you can also bring
your own keys,

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:49.470
and we don't have any
access to the data at all.

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:51.900
Don't lose the keys.

00:44:51.900 --> 00:44:53.214
This is serious.

00:44:53.214 --> 00:44:55.380
I mean, our guys are really
panicked about customers

00:44:55.380 --> 00:44:56.796
losing the keys
and then the media

00:44:56.796 --> 00:44:58.080
going Google lost the data.

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:04.020
And then we talked
about DLP API.

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:07.920
The example that I gave very
briefly this morning about you

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:11.190
as an employee organizing
a party for your children

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:13.350
through your corporate
email and then this

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:15.222
being archived in
the corporate archive

00:45:15.222 --> 00:45:16.680
and then coming
back 10 years ago--

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:19.710
10 years later and the
regulator asking your company

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:22.710
why did you keep the email
addresses of these children

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:27.210
in your email is a real question
we received from a customer.

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:28.620
This is the real situation.

00:45:28.620 --> 00:45:32.400
This is to tell how complex
this is going to become.

00:45:32.400 --> 00:45:37.050
And this is where advanced
technology can really help you.

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:40.080
And you had a demo
this morning, but this

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:47.580
is an animation of the DLP API
scanning through a live chat

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:50.340
box, also when you see
the feed coming in.

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:53.880
As the credit card
number is being typed,

00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:55.170
it is being flagged.

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:57.730
And so this technology
can be applied.

00:45:57.730 --> 00:46:00.210
You can think of plugging
this through our APIs

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:03.120
into your technology
and addressing

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:04.740
some of the big
challenges that are

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:06.480
coming-- that are coming ahead.

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:07.760
I want to summarize.

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:10.100
Who knows this tool?

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:12.240
All right, this is
Netherlands day.

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:14.160
You all knew-- you know
everything already.

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:18.950
So if you don't know this tool,
it's funny because there is--

00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:23.880
no, I'm not going to make
a regional distinction.

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:27.207
This is going the wrong way.

00:46:27.207 --> 00:46:29.040
If you don't know this
tool, go to this tool

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:31.670
as soon as you can, type
your email address in

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:34.850
and see how many times your
login credentials have been

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:36.950
put up for sale on the web.

00:46:36.950 --> 00:46:41.300
If they are out there and
you're reusing the password that

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:44.930
was associated to that login at
the time, you have a problem.

00:46:44.930 --> 00:46:47.120
You should not
reuse your password

00:46:47.120 --> 00:46:48.440
on different properties.

00:46:48.440 --> 00:46:52.400
To help you do that, install
Password Alert, which

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:55.340
is a Chrome extension on
Chrome that will automatically

00:46:55.340 --> 00:46:57.740
detect whenever you
reuse your password,

00:46:57.740 --> 00:47:00.492
and stop you from doing that.

00:47:00.492 --> 00:47:02.200
If you install this
in your organization,

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:04.757
in your enterprise, it will
also warn your administrator

00:47:04.757 --> 00:47:06.590
that you have reused
your corporate password

00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:08.464
for another property,
and it will immediately

00:47:08.464 --> 00:47:09.950
deactivate your password.

00:47:09.950 --> 00:47:12.820
At Google we don't change
passwords every 60, 90 days.

00:47:12.820 --> 00:47:15.090
I haven't changed my
passwords in many years

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:16.700
because I was
caught three times,

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:18.020
and I am not caught anymore.

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:20.240
I never reuse my
corporate passwords.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:22.400
I know it very well, and
that's what you need.

00:47:22.400 --> 00:47:25.880
And then lastly, second
step authentication--

00:47:25.880 --> 00:47:28.520
if you're not using second
step authentication today

00:47:28.520 --> 00:47:32.420
and you're still relying
on your passwords

00:47:32.420 --> 00:47:36.060
to protect you online, you're
not acting responsibly.

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:38.360
You should activate second
step authentication,

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:43.220
and you should activate
hardware based--

00:47:43.220 --> 00:47:44.630
I put the FIDO logo.

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:47.510
The FIDO is a global alliance
in which we participate

00:47:47.510 --> 00:47:50.090
that develop the
standards, and these FIDO

00:47:50.090 --> 00:47:53.660
enabled keys allow you to
bring a second factor that

00:47:53.660 --> 00:47:54.620
is un-fishable.

00:47:54.620 --> 00:47:56.690
It's basically a
cryptographic signature

00:47:56.690 --> 00:47:59.720
that leaves your
computer to the servers

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:02.090
as opposed to standard
two-step authentication

00:48:02.090 --> 00:48:03.594
when you have to type in a code.

00:48:03.594 --> 00:48:04.760
This code can be key logged.

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:07.800
This code can be captured by
a man in the middle attack.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:09.980
The second factor--
traditional second factor

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:14.180
has weaknesses that are being
abused increasingly now,

00:48:14.180 --> 00:48:16.190
and this is how we
protect Google now

00:48:16.190 --> 00:48:18.170
for the last four years.

00:48:18.170 --> 00:48:19.760
And this is available on Gmail.

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:21.590
You can order one
of those on Amazon,

00:48:21.590 --> 00:48:23.620
cost between $6 and $15.

00:48:23.620 --> 00:48:26.990
You receive it, you activate
it, you pair it to Gmail,

00:48:26.990 --> 00:48:29.690
and then you have a key for
your home, a key for your car,

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:31.200
and a key for your data.

00:48:31.200 --> 00:48:35.051
And you don't really mind
if your password disappears.

00:48:38.677 --> 00:48:39.260
Any questions?

00:48:39.260 --> 00:48:40.830
I don't know if we have
time for questions.

00:48:40.830 --> 00:48:42.204
We don't have time
for questions.

